Project name: DailyFlow

Project overview:
DailyFlow is a web application that helps users manage schedule, routines, and wellbeing in one place.

Core product areas:
- onboarding questionnaire
- calendar screen
- workouts
- meals and grocery planning
- stress and break recommendations
- overview / weekly summary
- profile settings

Main stack and infrastructure:
- Frontend: React + Vite
- Backend: Python on AWS Lambda
- Authentication: Amazon Cognito
- Database: DynamoDB
- Hosting: AWS Amplify
- External integration: Google Calendar
- User-uploaded assets: Amazon S3
- AI integration: OpenAI

Existing DynamoDB tables:
- Users
  - PK: user_id
- Integrations
  - PK: user_id
- BusyBlocks
  - PK: user_id
  - SK: block_key
- DailyFlowEvents
  - PK: user_id
  - SK: event_id
- WorkoutLibrary
  - PK: user_id

Current implemented foundation:
- Cognito sign up / log in works.
- Onboarding questionnaire exists.
- Profile/settings foundation exists.
- Profile image flow exists and uses S3.
- Google Calendar OAuth flow exists.
- Google Calendar list fetch exists.
- User-selected Google calendars are persisted.
- BusyBlocks sync/get flows exist.
- Calendar screen supports DAY / WEEK / MONTH.
- A dedicated Google Calendar named DailyFlow is created/reused for app-created events.
- DailyFlow calendar id is stored in Integrations as dailyflow_calendar_id.
- Workouts screen exists and is connected to real backend/OpenAI/Google Calendar flows.

Google Calendar rules:
- Do not create duplicate DailyFlow calendars.
- Before creating a DailyFlow calendar, always check whether one already exists and reuse it.
- DailyFlow-created events should be created only inside the dedicated DailyFlow Google Calendar.
- BusyBlocks and DailyFlow-created events are different concepts and must remain logically separated.
- BusyBlocks should represent occupied time from the user-selected Google calendars.
- Reuse existing Google OAuth/token refresh logic where possible.
- Current Google scopes should preserve both:
  - https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.readonly
  - https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.app.created

Profile/settings rules:
- Screens should use the shared profile/preferences source.
- Do not create duplicate local preference state if existing profile data can be reused.
- Preferences changed in one screen should be reflected in other screens after save/navigation.
- Avoid adding new onboarding questionnaire questions unless explicitly requested.
- Feature-specific preferences may live inside that feature when appropriate.

Workouts rules:
- Workouts implementation should remain stable and not be rewritten unless explicitly requested.
- Workout generation should use saved preferences and available free time.
- Existing saved/approved workout plan items should not disappear after Generate.
- Items added to Google Calendar should remain marked as added.
- Add to calendar must not create duplicate Google events.
- Delete should remove the linked DailyFlow-created event when applicable.
- Do not change Workouts while working on Meals unless the requested task explicitly depends on shared code.

Current active milestone:
The current active milestone is Meals & Grocery.

Meals & Grocery high-level direction:
- Build the real Meals & Grocery screen, not a temporary test page.
- The screen should include:
  - Meal Library
  - Saved Meals This Week
  - Grocery List
- There should not be an automatic weekly meal plan like Workouts.
- The user manually chooses meals from the Meal Library.
- Clicking + on a meal should open a popup where the user chooses date and time.
- The app should calculate the end time using the meal prep duration.
- The app should check BusyBlocks before saving a meal prep event.
- If valid, the app should create a DailyFlow Google Calendar event.
- Saved Meals This Week should be the source for Grocery List generation.
- Grocery List should not be generated from the entire Meal Library.
- Meals should support servings, and grocery quantities should update according to servings.
- Grocery list quantities should be aggregated and rounded sensibly for package-like units.
- Meal Library should support favorites and filters.
- Meal Preferences should be feature-specific and should not extend the onboarding questionnaire unless explicitly requested.
- Use existing profile/questionnaire data where relevant, for example diet-related preferences.

Planned Meals DynamoDB table:
- Prefer one focused table:
  - MealsAndGroceries
  - PK: user_id
  - SK: record_key
- Exact schema/details should be defined in the relevant implementation prompt, not assumed too early.

General milestone rules:
- Work incrementally and in small scoped steps.
- Do not skip ahead to unrelated milestone work.
- Do not implement Stress, Overview, Apple Watch, or unrelated features unless explicitly requested.
- Do not rewrite unrelated parts of the app.
- Do not add unnecessary architecture changes.
- Keep Calendar, Workouts, Meals, Stress, and Overview logically separated.
- Reuse shared infrastructure when appropriate:
  - profile/preferences
  - Google Calendar
  - BusyBlocks
  - DailyFlow calendar
  - DailyFlowEvents

Implementation guidance:
- Keep code simple and minimal.
- Do not over-engineer.
- Prefer readable components and small focused functions.
- Do not add unnecessary libraries.
- Use English for code, file names, comments, and prompts.
- Reuse the existing project structure as much as possible.
- Only create or update files required for the requested step.
- Keep backend routes minimal and product-oriented.
- Avoid temporary routes or throwaway AI/testing endpoints.
- If placeholder logic is used, keep it light and easy to replace.
- Use real product routes when backend work is requested.

AWS / deployment guidance:
- If a change requires AWS updates, state them explicitly and concretely.
- If a change requires Google Cloud / Google OAuth updates, state them explicitly and concretely.
- If a new DynamoDB table is required, list:
  - table name
  - partition key
  - sort key if any
  - required Lambda IAM permissions
  - required environment variables
- If code changes require only redeploy and no AWS config changes, say that clearly.
- Do not write “AWS CHANGES REQUIRED: none” when frontend or Lambda redeploy is still required.
- Distinguish between:
  - AWS configuration changes
  - deployment/redeploy requirements

Working style:
- Before generating code, first explain the plan.
- Before generating code, list which files will be created or updated.
- Keep changes scoped to the requested step only.
- Prefer production-oriented structure, but implement only the smallest correct piece for the requested step.
- At the end of every response that includes code changes, include:
  - exact files changed
  - short summary
  - manual test steps
  - AWS CHANGES REQUIRED
- In AWS CHANGES REQUIRED, explain only what must change because of the actual code changes.
- Do not give generic AWS advice.
- If no AWS changes and no deploy are needed, write exactly:
  AWS CHANGES REQUIRED: none